import ui "sngl:ui"
import tree "sngl:tree"

// The tree the inline pieces form. Declaring it here is what makes `span` a
// name a component can resolve, so a misspelling is an unresolved name rather
// than a family nobody joins.
//
// A member is a run of text and what is true of it. It hosts its own family
// and nothing else: emphasis around a link is a link inside emphasis, and both
// orders are one flow of words. What it may not hold is a `node` -- a box
// placed in the middle of a sentence has no line to sit on -- which is the
// membership rule doing the work a content model would otherwise do in prose.
#[tree.kind]
struct span {}

// Shows one flow of rich text. Its rest slot accepts spans, so it hosts them
// without being one, and the flow wraps as a whole: a line break falls
// wherever the words put it, not at the edge of a child.
//
// `role` says what the run is for and `style` says what it looks like. The
// first is the one to reach for: a platform that has headings emits one, with
// the host's own typography, and `style` is how an author says something
// different from that. A span inside says what is different about its own
// words and inherits the rest.
//
// One run is one paragraph. A document is a `vbox` of these -- usually of the
// block components at the foot of this file, which are each a `richText` with
// its role already set -- and what goes between them is everything a document
// holds that this family does not describe.
component richText(
    style ui.Style,
    role Role,
    content ...component span,
) ui.node

// What a run of text is *for*, as opposed to what it looks like.
//
// A platform answers it however its host does: html emits the element the
// role names, so a heading announces as one and a quote is quoted; fyne has a
// paragraph style per role; a terminal has the styling and none of the
// semantics. Which is the reason this is a prop on the one primitive rather
// than a component per role -- a component hosting the span family is a
// primitive by construction (`hostsTree`), so an override of one would not
// inline and would land on an emitter that has never heard of it. As a prop it
// reaches every emitter through the node they already render.
//
// Meaning only -- the appearance is `style`, set beside it by whichever block
// component wrapped this run. Leaving the typography to each platform's
// reading of the role was the alternative, and it makes a new target look
// wrong until it has implemented six heading sizes; setting it here means a
// target implements escaping and inherits a document that reads correctly.
// A host that has its own idea about a heading is free to let the role win.
enum Role { paragraph, heading, quote, code, caption }

// A literal run of text -- the leaf every other span eventually holds.
//
// Literal means every character of it, spaces and newlines included. Two
// spaces are two spaces and a newline ends the line, on every target, because
// what an author wrote between the quotes is the one thing a document cannot
// afford to have a platform reinterpret. Wrapping still happens around that:
// the run flows to the width it is given, and an explicit newline breaks it
// wherever it falls.
//
// So the collapsing html does to whitespace is html's to undo -- a `pre-wrap`
// on the run, or the entities and breaks that say the same thing where a style
// cannot be attached -- and it is codegen's job rather than the author's. The
// other targets carry the characters through as written.
//
// Named `text` rather than for the package: a program writes
// `markup.text("...")`, which is the spelling that reads, and a program
// dot-importing both this package and `sngl:ui` is told about the collision
// where it wrote it.
component text(value string) span

// A run of text and what is true of its appearance -- the one span that
// carries style, and what every wrapper below is written in terms of.
//
// `style` says what `ui.Style` has a word for: weight, slant, family, size,
// color, alignment. `underline` and `strike` are props because it does not --
// there is no `textDecoration` field, and inventing one would add a property
// to every component in the language to serve two spans.
//
// Nothing about a run says what it *means*. `token` says that for code and
// `role` says it for a whole flow; this is appearance, and a document that
// reaches for it directly is saying it wants those exact letters rather than
// what the wrappers name.
component run(
    style ui.Style,
    underline bool,
    strike bool,
    content ...component span,
) span

// --- The named weights and faces ---
//
// Each is a `run` with one thing set, which is what makes them worth having:
// a document says `bold` and means the word carries weight, and the one place
// that decides what carrying weight looks like is here.
//
// Convenience, not override points. A span-hosting component is a primitive by
// construction (`hostsTree`), so these compose away into the `run` they wrap
// but a platform *override* of one would not inline -- a target that needs to
// do something particular about bold does it in its `run` emitter, reading the
// style it was handed.

// The word carries weight.
component bold(content ...component span) span {
    run(style={fontWeight=ui.FontWeight.bold}) {
        content
    }
}

// The word is slanted, using the family's italic face where one exists.
component italic(content ...component span) span {
    run(style={fontStyle=ui.FontStyle.italic}) {
        content
    }
}

// A rule under the words.
component underline(content ...component span) span {
    run(underline=true) {
        content
    }
}

// A rule through the words: text that was there and is not any more.
component strike(content ...component span) span {
    run(strike=true) {
        content
    }
}

// A run set in a monospaced face -- an identifier, a flag, a filename, a line
// of a code sample.
//
// Its content is spans rather than a string, which is what makes a highlighted
// code sample writable: the coloring is `token` spans inside this one. A leaf
// taking a string could only ever be one color, so the two decisions are the
// same decision.
component monospace(content ...component span) span {
    run(style={fontFamily="monospace"}) {
        content
    }
}

// What a run of code *is*, for a platform to color as it sees fit.
//
// Semantic rather than a color, because the alternative does not survive the
// trip. A document that names #7DAEA3 is unreadable on a light theme and
// approximate on a terminal with sixteen colors, and it overrides an
// application that had its own palette. A kind is answered by whoever is
// drawing: a CSS class on html, a theme color name on fyne, an ANSI pair in a
// terminal, a Pango foreground on gtk4.
//
// `plain` is the default and is what a run that is code but is nothing in
// particular gets: whitespace, an identifier a tokenizer did not classify, the
// whole of a fence whose language nobody highlights. Written rather than left
// out, so a highlighter says "this is plain" and a reader of the tree is never
// asked to tell that from a missing span.
//
// The set is otherwise deliberately small -- what every syntax theme
// distinguishes, and no more. A highlighter with a finer vocabulary maps down
// to these, which is the direction that loses nothing a reader can see.
enum Token { plain, keyword, type, function, variable, constant, string, number, comment, operator, punctuation }

// The content is a run of code of this kind. Written inside a `monospace`,
// which is what says it is code at all; this says which part of it.
component token(kind Token, content ...component span) span

// The words lead somewhere. `href` takes the same URL a `ui.link` does, and
// the content is spans, so a link may be emphasized and emphasis may be
// linked.
component link(href string, content ...component span) span

// A picture on the line with the words -- an icon, a badge, an inline
// formula. Its bare children are the description: what the picture shows, for
// a reader who is not looking at it and for a platform that cannot draw one
// inside a flow of text.
//
// Spans rather than a string because that is what a description is in
// CommonMark, where the text between the brackets is inline content like any
// other. A platform whose description is a plain attribute -- html's `alt` --
// flattens it to its words, which is a thing codegen can do and a thing an
// author writing the markup cannot undo.
component image(src string, alt ...component span) span

// --- Document blocks ---
//
// Ordinary `ui.node` components with bodies, not members of the family: a
// document is a `vbox` of these, and each is a `richText` that says what it
// is. They are here rather than in an application because the semantic is not
// a style an author should have to remember -- `heading(level=2)` announces as
// a heading on the targets that have the concept, and is a bold larger line on
// the ones that do not.
//
// Each sets both halves: a `role`, which is what the block means, and a
// `style`, which is what it looks like where the host has no opinion. The
// sizes are the ones a document outline wants rather than any platform's, and
// an author who disagrees wraps `richText` directly.
//
// Bodied on purpose. Each composes away into the `richText` it wraps, so a
// platform implements the one primitive and inherits all of these; there is no
// per-block override to write and none to forget.

// A document's own title -- the one heading a page has at most one of.
//
// The six levels are six components rather than one taking a level, for the
// reason `Role` gives: a level is only ever one of six, and a number can be
// none of them.
component heading1(content ...component span) ui.node {
    richText(role=Role.heading1, style={fontSize=32px, fontWeight=ui.FontWeight.bold}) {
        content
    }
}

// A section title.
component heading2(content ...component span) ui.node {
    richText(role=Role.heading2, style={fontSize=26px, fontWeight=ui.FontWeight.bold}) {
        content
    }
}

// A subsection title.
component heading3(content ...component span) ui.node {
    richText(role=Role.heading3, style={fontSize=21px, fontWeight=ui.FontWeight.bold}) {
        content
    }
}

// A title below a subsection.
component heading4(content ...component span) ui.node {
    richText(role=Role.heading4, style={fontSize=18px, fontWeight=ui.FontWeight.bold}) {
        content
    }
}

// A title four levels down; rare outside a reference document.
component heading5(content ...component span) ui.node {
    richText(role=Role.heading5, style={fontSize=16px, fontWeight=ui.FontWeight.bold}) {
        content
    }
}

// The deepest title a document outline has.
component heading6(content ...component span) ui.node {
    richText(role=Role.heading6, style={fontSize=14px, fontWeight=ui.FontWeight.bold}) {
        content
    }
}

// A run of prose -- the block a paragraph of a document becomes, and the
// default.
component paragraph(content ...component span) ui.node {
    richText(role=Role.paragraph) {
        content
    }
}

// Words quoted from somewhere else.
component quote(content ...component span) ui.node {
    richText(role=Role.quote, style={fontStyle=ui.FontStyle.italic, paddingLeft=12px, borderLeft="3px solid #CCCCCC"}) {
        content
    }
}

// A code sample on its own, as opposed to a `monospace` run inside a
// sentence. Its content is spans, so it holds the `token` spans a highlighter
// produced; its whitespace is literal like every other run.
//
// It wraps them in `monospace` itself, because a code block is monospaced by
// being one -- a caller that had to say so could forget to, and a highlighter
// emitting token spans has no reason to know it is being rendered as a block.
component codeBlock(content ...component span) ui.node {
    richText(role=Role.code, style={padding=12px, background=#F5F5F5, borderRadius=4px}) {
        monospace {
            content
        }
    }
}

// A caption: what the thing above or below it is. Smaller and quieter than
// prose on every host that distinguishes them.
component caption(content ...component span) ui.node {
    richText(role=Role.caption, style={fontSize=13px, color=#666666}) {
        content
    }
}

// Whether a list item is a task, and if so whether it is done. Three states
// rather than a `bool`, because "not a task at all" is the common one and a
// flag cannot say it: a checkbox is a thing a markdown list item may have and
// usually does not.
enum Task { none, todo, done }

// A list, and one item of one. Their own components rather than a role,
// because a list is a construct the host has -- `<ul>` announces its length
// and its position to a screen reader, and a run of text styled with a bullet
// announces neither.
//
// They host nodes and not spans, which is what makes them the two blocks a
// platform may usefully override: a component hosting the span family is a
// primitive by construction, and these are not, so an override of either
// inlines the way every `sngl:ui` override does. It is also faithful -- an
// item of a markdown list holds blocks, so a nested list and a code sample go
// in one, and the indent accumulates because each list adds its own.
//
// `ordered` and `start` are for whoever emits a real list; the default body
// does not read them, because it cannot count.
component list(ordered bool = false, start int = 1, items ...component) ui.node {
    ui.vbox(style={gap=4, paddingLeft=20px}) {
        items
    }
}

// One item. `marker` is the bullet or the number, written by whoever knows the
// ordinal rather than counted at render time: a platform that emits a real
// list item ignores it and lets the host number the list, and a platform with
// no list construct shows it. That is what lets an ordered list be numbered on
// a target that cannot count, and it is how a nested list gets a different
// bullet from its parent.
//
// Empty is the plain bullet. Deriving one per depth from a context is what
// this wants and is not what it does: a context provider whose value reads the
// context it overrides does not accumulate, and emits the context name as a
// bare identifier nothing declares. Until that is fixed, depth is the
// importer's to know -- which it does, along with the ordinal.
//
// `task` replaces the marker with a checkbox, which is what a markdown task
// list is. It is not interactive: a document describes state rather than
// holding it, and an application that wants a real checkbox writes one.
component listItem(
    marker string = "",
    task Task = Task.none,
    content ...component,
) ui.node {
    ui.hbox(style={gap=8, alignItems=ui.Alignment.start}) {
        if task == Task.none {
            ui.text(value=marker == "" ? "•" : marker)
        } else {
            ui.checkbox(checked=task == Task.done, disabled=true)
        }
        ui.vbox {
            content
        }
    }
}
